y let me reconsider which is \"the Goomba's entry\". The table entry is 6 bytes FC FC 70 71 72 73. The task says \"'one of those bytes'\". So changing 0x6794 is fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: if the game copies the tile list to RAM at spawn, the changed byte takes effect on respawn after reset. Good.\n\nHowever, I realize a potential problem: 0x6792 might not be the Goomba entry at all but part of another table. But the sequence match is strong.\n\nLet me also consider the possibility that the game reads the tile list from a *different* table with the same values, but search found only one occurrence. So it must be this.\n\nBut the read trace found nothing... Let me test differently: do a trace_writes READ on CPU $E73E (start of the region, the FC FC AA AB...) for 60 frames while a Goomba is active but off-screen? That won't help.\n\nActually, maybe the read trace uses CPU address space but the game reads via a pointer in zero page with a *different* base. E.g., the table could be at CPU $E700 but the game reads the tile values through a RAM buffer it filled at level load. If cached, no ROM reads during play.\n\nOK let me just do the write proof. If the Goomba isn't on screen after reset, I'll need to reach one.\n\nActually, an easier and more reliable way to display the Goomba after reset: after reset, do the Start/Start to 1-1, then hold right until a Goomba appears. That worked before (we saw one at OAM 52-57).\n\nLet me now:\n1. In hack eval-sheet2-d (active), write_memory prgRom 0x6794 = 0x00 with reason.\n2. reset_emulator.\n3. play to 1-1 and scroll until Goomba on screen.\n4. pause, screenshot, list_sprites to show the changed tile.\n5. undo_edit.\n\nFor step 3, I can use the same play script that worked: [{start,6},{},60},{start,6},{},90},{right,110}] but that ended with Goomba active. Actually earlier the run [{start,6},{},60},{start,6},{},90},{right,110}] left us in mode 1 with no active enemy (all $000F=0) but Mario at x114. Then a further right 130 spawned the Goomba. So combined, ~240 frames of right from start gets a Goomba. But then it was off-screen when I paused later? Actually at the end of the 130-frame run the Goomba was at x47 and Mario x24 and $000F=1, but list_sprites showed no Goomba. Weird — maybe the Goomba's page made it offscreen (Mario wrapped the page). \n\nHmm. Actually wait: during that 130-frame run, Mario's x went 115...247, then wrapped to 0, 24. So Mario crossed into page 1 then? No, $0086 wrapped 251->0 means Mario's page $006D incremented to 1. So Mario absolute x = 256+24 = 280. The Goomba low byte 47 with page maybe 1 -> absolute x ~303. Camera follows Mario. Goomba at screen x = 303 - camera. Camera maybe ~168 -> screen ~135. So the Goomba should be visible! But list_sprites showed none. Unless the Goomba got stomped/despawned in the final frames. $000F last sample was 1 but the run continued past the last sample? The last sample frame 129, run ended frame 130. Hmm.\n\nActually list_sprites filters \"only on-screen\" sprites, and the Goomba's OAM entries might be blanked if it's off the visible area. Or the Goomba may have been at the screen edge.\n\nThis is getting expensive. Let me take a robust approach: after reset, use play_input to reach the Goomba, then immediately pause and inspect. If no Goomba, walk a bit more.\n\nActually, even more robust: I can force a Goomba active and on-screen via RAM writes after reaching the area: set $000F slot0=1, $0016=6, $0087=150, $006E=0 (page), $00CF=184. Then step a frame and the game will draw it. Wait, writing $000F=1 makes the slot active; the sprite-drawing routine will read the tile list for it. But does the Goomba drawing need other state? The mapping's $000F proof: writing 0 removed the Goomba. Writing 1 might resurrect it. Let's try: at 1-1 start (or wherever), set $000F=1, $0016=6, $0087=150, $006E=0, $00CF=184, and the Goomba should appear at screen x = 150 - camera.\n\nActually simpler: the game will process and move it. Fine.\n\n